const String foundingBaldursGate = '''
Centuries ago, the hero Balduran spent years questing in lands across the Sea of Swords and beyond. When he finally returned to his village of Gray Harbor, he brought fantastic wealth with him, much of which he gifted to friends and family.

These boons greatly improved Gray Harbor's fortunes, launching businesses, expanding its docks, and inspiring the creation of a defensive wall around the town. In honor of their heroic patron, the citizens came to call one of their new wall's passages Baldur's Gate.

Within a generation, the gate became synonymous with the community itself, and the settlement known as Baldur's Gate began appearing on maps throughout the Sword Coast.

The city's surprising growth attracted all manner of people. Peasants affected by raiding and war, farmers rendered penniless by famine and drought, pirates seeking a neutral port, and countless others all saw a chance to make their mark on the rapidly growing community.
''';

const String orderInBaldursGate = '''
As the city swelled, questions of law and taxation arose. The community's eldest families, largely those wealthy enough to afford homes within the city walls, came to be known as patriars and grew wary of the influx of strangers settling beyond their walls.

The creation of various additional taxes on trade and travel led to violence between the Upper City and the Lower City. Patriar houses were ransacked, family heirlooms were lost, heirs were kidnapped, and mercenary companies were hired to protect powerful interests.

Only the election of a new group of rulers, known as the dukes and collectively called the Council of Four, brought the unrest to an end. These elected rulers have remained part of the city's government ever since.

Another pillar of order emerged when the adventurer Eltan returned home after years abroad. Seeing the chaos that had overtaken his beloved city, he united the independent mercenary companies beneath a single banner: the Flaming Fist.

Using these forces, Eltan suppressed open disorder and punished criminals. Though crime continued behind closed doors, the creation of the Flaming Fist marked the beginning of a new era in Baldur's Gate.
''';

const String bhaalspawn = '''
Over the years, Baldur's Gate has harbored countless conspiracies and evil schemes. Perhaps the most infamous involved Bhaal, the god of murder.

Foreseeing his own death during the Time of Troubles, Bhaal conceived many mortal offspring. His plan was for these children, known as Bhaalspawn, to eventually restore him. They hunted and slew one another, with survivors absorbing ever more of Bhaal's divine essence.

One such Bhaalspawn, Sarevok, became a member of the Iron Throne merchant organization and sought to provoke war with Amn in order to ascend as the new Lord of Murder.

Another Bhaalspawn, aided by powerful allies, thwarted Sarevok's ambitions and restored a measure of stability to the city.

Despite this victory, Bhaal and his followers have continued to menace Baldur's Gate ever since.
''';

const String baldursGateToday = '''
This gazetteer describes Baldur's Gate as it exists in 1492 DR.

The original wall surrounding the Upper City still stands, while a second defensive wall now encloses the Lower City. Beyond these walls lies the Outer City, a sprawling collection of hastily built structures and shantytowns stretching along the River Chionthar.

Every district possesses its own dangers.

In the Upper City, patriar families and government officials engage in endless political maneuvering. Blackmail, sabotage, fabricated scandals, and covert investigations are common tools in the struggle for power.

The Lower City serves as the city's turbulent middle ground. Crime, social inequality, foreign threats, and economic pressures leave many citizens feeling trapped. The Flaming Fist often responds to problems with excessive force, causing residents to organize into local crews for protection.

The Outer City is a place where refugees, wanderers, traders, and criminals from across Faerûn mingle. Communities rise and fall constantly, and foreign customs and faiths flourish with little oversight.

Baldur's Gate generally follows a "do no harm" policy regarding religions and organizations. Any group may operate openly so long as influential citizens are not harmed and commerce is not disrupted.

Despite its corruption and violence, Baldur's Gate remains a city of opportunity. Adventurers can find wealthy patrons, hunt criminals for rewards, uncover conspiracies, join secret societies, or protect ordinary citizens.

Information, treasures, secrets, influence, and even souls can be bought or sold for the right price.

As one famous Baldurian hero once said:

"Watch your step in the shadows. Watch your back in the light. Win a prize beyond your wildest dreams or disappear into forgotten history. Every day your life is on the line. Every day you could become a legend. Welcome to Baldur's Gate."
''';

const String neighborsRivalsAndAllies = '''
Other cities and nations closely monitor Baldur's Gate as it grows in wealth, influence, and power.

To the north, Baldur's Gate maintains trade relationships with Waterdeep and Neverwinter. To the south lies Amn, a powerful mercantile nation that seeks dominance over trade along the Sea of Swords and views Baldur's Gate as a dangerous competitor.

To counter Amnian ambitions, Baldur's Gate joined the Lords' Alliance alongside cities such as Waterdeep and Neverwinter. Should Amn ever attack, Baldur's Gate can expect military support from its allies.

To the east, the nations of Elturgard and Cormyr regard Baldur's Gate as a lawless and morally questionable city. They are especially critical of its broad religious tolerance and permissive attitudes toward many organizations and faiths.
''';

const String candlekeep = '''
Candlekeep, the greatest library in Faerûn, requires visitors to present a book not already contained within its archives before entry is granted.

Because of this unusual requirement, travelers often visit Baldur's Gate in search of rare books before continuing south to Candlekeep.

The city therefore supports a thriving trade in books and manuscripts. Certain merchants are known for purchasing valuable books without asking questions and then reselling them at enormous profit.

Adventurers are frequently hired to recover rare texts from ruins, forgotten tombs, and dangerous wilderness locations.

When information cannot be found through Baldur's Gate's markets and secret networks, Candlekeep becomes the next destination. Scholars and travelers often hire guards and adventurers to escort them and their precious books safely along the road.

The Guild is well aware of this traffic and frequently targets travelers heading toward Candlekeep, stealing valuable books for resale within the city.
''';

const String elturel = '''
Before the events of this adventure, Elturel stood as the capital of Elturgard on the upper reaches of the River Chionthar.

Where Baldur's Gate is a city of mercenaries, intrigue, and commerce, Elturel was known as a lawful and devout city protected by knights devoted to Helm, Torm, Tyr, and Lathander.

The two cities could hardly be more different.

Whenever Baldur's Gate attempted to expand its influence in ways Elturgard disliked, Elturel often retaliated by imposing heavy taxes on river traffic traveling downstream.

Economic competition and dramatically different religious philosophies created longstanding tension between the cities, though neither side was eager to test the military strength of the other.
''';

const String portNyanzaru = '''
Baldur's Gate serves as one of the primary ports from which ships depart for Chult, the distant southern peninsula.

Port Nyanzaru, located on Chult's northern coast, enjoys a profitable and generally friendly trade relationship with Baldur's Gate. Both cities share a dislike of Amn and benefit from mutual commerce.

Beyond trade with Port Nyanzaru itself, Baldur's Gate maintains holdings in Chult. The most significant is Fort Beluarian, a Flaming Fist stronghold that serves as a military outpost and staging area for exploration.

The fort oversees expeditions, resource extraction, trade ventures, and less public activities carried out in Baldur's Gate's interests.

As a result, exotic treasures and rare goods from deep within Chult frequently appear in the markets of Baldur's Gate.

Travel between the two regions remains dangerous. Pirates, Amnian privateers, and sea monsters threaten shipping routes throughout the voyage.
''';

const String government = '''
The Parliament of Peers is a legislative body composed primarily of nearly fifty patriar representatives. It debates matters of law, taxation, trade, and governance before presenting recommendations to the city's rulers.

When a majority agreement is reached, the parliament submits its position to the Council of Four for final consideration.

The Council of Four consists of three dukes and one grand duke. Council members vote on matters of state, and in the event of a tie, the grand duke's vote counts twice.

Many citizens believe bribery and political corruption are widespread throughout the government. Officials rarely accept direct payments openly. Instead, bribes are often disguised as purchases of inexpensive artwork, jewelry, or other goods that secretly contain valuable gems or platinum.

Among Baldurians, political influence is often considered just as valuable as wealth, and successful manipulation of the city's social networks is viewed as a mark of competence.
''';

const String councilOfFour = '''
Comprised of four dukes, the Council of Four presides over the government of Baldur's Gate.

Although the Parliament of Peers and the patriars wield considerable influence, the Council of Four remains the city's highest authority.

The council meets within High Hall, a heavily fortified government complex located in the Upper City.

Its responsibilities include command of the Flaming Fist, oversight of the city's navy, supervision of tax collection, management of public utilities, and administration of countless civic functions.

The council consists of one Grand Duke and three Dukes, each possessing significant political power. Despite this authority, rivalries, corruption, and personal ambitions frequently complicate governance.
''';

const String grandDukeUlderRavengard = '''
Ulder Ravengard is a fearless soldier who rose through the ranks of the Flaming Fist to become its Supreme Marshal.

Using his military reputation and political influence, Ravengard secured a seat on the Council of Four. Following the deaths of two council members amid scandal and corruption, he successfully convinced the Parliament of Peers to support his election as Grand Duke.

After assuming the highest office in Baldur's Gate, Ravengard refused to surrender command of the Flaming Fist. This decision made him the single most powerful individual in the city.

Ravengard cares little for popularity. His primary concerns are the stability, prosperity, and security of Baldur's Gate. He distrusts many members of both the Council and Parliament, believing they often place personal interests above the welfare of the city.

Supported largely by commoners and reform-minded citizens, Ravengard entered office promising practical leadership and resistance to corruption. However, bureaucracy and political opposition have limited many of his efforts.

Despite these obstacles, he remains one of the few respected voices of reason within the city's government.

Recently, Ravengard was lured to a diplomatic summit in Elturel through a fiendish political scheme orchestrated by his enemies. During his absence, the Flaming Fist is left without its leader and the Council of Four struggles without its strongest figure.
''';

const String dukeBelynneStelmane = '''
Once a brilliant and formidable politician, Duke Belynne Stelmane suffered what most citizens believe was a devastating seizure.

In truth, her condition resulted from the mental domination of a mind flayer.

The attack left Stelmane partially paralyzed and severely affected her speech. Although she remains aware of her situation, she wages a silent internal struggle against the creature's influence.

Even her closest aides remain unaware of the true nature of her affliction, though they work tirelessly to protect her reputation and compensate for her declining condition.

Because of this secret battle, Stelmane is largely unable to resist the political maneuvering of her fellow dukes. As other council members compete for influence, she remains trapped between maintaining appearances and fighting for control of her own mind.
''';

const String dukeDillardPortyr = '''
Duke Dillard Portyr was once a respected businessman and investor. After suffering a series of unfortunate financial ventures, he gradually withdrew from active commerce and embraced a quieter life.

Now wealthy and elderly, Portyr spends much of his time enjoying the comforts afforded by his title and fortune.

Having outlived both his wives and all three of his sons, he lives largely alone. One of his closest remaining relatives is his niece, Liara Portyr, commander of Fort Beluarian in Chult.

Portyr is famously conflict-averse. He prefers avoiding difficult decisions whenever possible and often responds to problems by promising to investigate them rather than taking direct action.

Although polite, sympathetic, and personable, he is frequently manipulated by stronger political personalities.

Many citizens view him as ineffective, believing he listens carefully, makes sincere promises, and then accomplishes very little.

Despite his reluctance to act, Portyr expressed concern regarding Ravengard's diplomatic trip to Elturel. If Baldur's Gate descends into chaos during Ravengard's absence, Portyr intends to request that his niece return from Chult to assume temporary command of the Flaming Fist.
''';

const String dukeThalamraVanthampur = '''
Acid-tongued, ambitious, and exceptionally shrewd, Duke Thalamra Vanthampur serves as the matriarch of the influential Vanthampur family.

Unlike many patriars, she was not born into wealth. Her rise to power began in the city's sewer and infrastructure departments, where she worked her way upward through years of determination, manipulation, and political maneuvering.

Promotion followed promotion until she eventually became Master of Drains and Underways. During this time she married three times and raised three sons.

Her lifelong ambition has always been to elevate both herself and her family into the highest circles of Baldurian society.

Years of calculated alliances, strategic bribery, and careful blame shifting ultimately earned her a seat on the Council of Four.

Although she has served on the council for less time than the other dukes, Vanthampur is widely regarded as the most politically skilled member.

She rarely speaks during official meetings, preferring to conduct business behind closed doors in private offices and family estates.

When she does speak publicly, however, her words carry unusual force. She is known for devastating insults, sharp observations, and an uncanny ability to expose uncomfortable truths.

Many political observers consider her the most dangerous individual on the Council of Four.
''';

const String military = '''
Baldur's Gate maintains two primary military and law enforcement organizations: the Flaming Fist and the Watch.

Together, these forces are responsible for protecting the city, enforcing laws, maintaining order, and responding to emergencies.

The two organizations operate in different districts and serve different social classes, reflecting the deep divisions within Baldur's Gate itself.
''';

const String flamingFist = '''
The Flaming Fist is the most recognizable military force in Baldur's Gate.

Originally formed by the legendary warrior Eltan, the organization united numerous independent mercenary companies beneath a single banner.

Today the Flaming Fist functions as the city's standing army and primary law enforcement force throughout most districts.

Funded by the Council of Four and commanded by Grand Duke Ulder Ravengard, the organization patrols the Lower City and exercises authority throughout the Outer City as well.

Thousands serve within its ranks, including native Baldurians, immigrants, former criminals, and retired adventurers.

The Flaming Fist often values efficiency over fairness. Citizens frequently criticize its members for excessive force, harsh punishments, and indifference toward collateral damage.

Despite these flaws, the organization remains one of the few barriers preventing Baldur's Gate from descending completely into chaos.
''';

const String watch = '''
The Watch serves as the official security force of the Upper City.

Unlike the Flaming Fist, the Watch is funded primarily by the patriars and focuses almost exclusively on protecting the city's wealthy elite.

Its officers are disciplined, orderly, and highly regimented. Patrol schedules are predictable and carefully maintained.

At dusk, the Watch clears the Upper City of everyone except residents, servants, and officially invited guests.

Many officers pride themselves on recognizing every Upper City resident by sight and quickly identifying outsiders.

The organization operates from the Citadel, a massive fortress built directly into the Upper City's defenses.

Although often criticized as little more than bodyguards for the rich, the Watch remains an important pillar of stability within Baldur's Gate.
''';

const String lawAndOrder = '''
All citizens of Baldur's Gate are expected to obey common law.

Crimes such as murder, theft, assault, blackmail, fraud, and conspiracy carry severe punishments. However, enforcement is rarely equal.

Patriars and politically connected individuals often receive lenient treatment, while commoners face harsher penalties for similar offenses.

Both the Watch and the Flaming Fist possess authority to deliver immediate justice when witnessing crimes directly.

In more complicated cases, suspects are imprisoned until trial. Wealthy individuals are often placed under house arrest, while commoners remain in jail.

Minor crimes usually result in fines, public humiliation, flogging, or time in the stocks.

The legal profession is controlled by the Barrister's Guild. Because lawyers are expensive, they generally represent wealthy clients.

Poor citizens frequently have little choice but to plead their own cases or hire adventurers to gather evidence on their behalf.

As with many aspects of life in Baldur's Gate, justice often depends as much on wealth and influence as it does on truth.
''';

const String citizenry = '''
The citizens of Baldur's Gate come from every corner of Faerûn and beyond. Merchants, sailors, mercenaries, laborers, artisans, refugees, criminals, and adventurers all contribute to the city's vibrant but volatile character.

Most residents spend their lives balancing ambition against danger. Wealth and success are possible, but so are exploitation, violence, and betrayal.

The city's social divisions are stark. The wealthy patriars live behind guarded walls in the Upper City, skilled workers and merchants populate the Lower City, and the poor struggle to survive in the Outer City.

Despite these inequalities, many citizens believe Baldur's Gate offers opportunities unavailable elsewhere. Hard work, cleverness, luck, or ruthlessness can elevate a person from obscurity to prominence.
''';

const String commonersAndCrews = '''
Many common folk feel abandoned by both the government and the Flaming Fist. As a result, residents often form local organizations known as crews.

A crew is part neighborhood association, part extended family, and part mutual defense society. Members help one another find work, settle disputes, share resources, and defend against criminals.

Crews often organize festivals, maintain local traditions, and provide aid during hardships. Membership is usually based on neighborhood ties, family relationships, occupation, or long-standing friendships.

Although crews are technically informal organizations, many possess significant influence within their communities.

The strongest crews can rival criminal organizations in local authority, though most focus on protecting their members rather than seeking power.

Conflicts between crews occasionally erupt into street fights, but such disputes are usually resolved before attracting the attention of the Flaming Fist.
''';

const String noteworthyLaws = '''
While countless regulations govern life in Baldur's Gate, several laws stand out due to their importance and unusual nature.

Citizens are forbidden from carrying plague victims into the city without authorization.

Unauthorized use of magic to influence public officials is considered a serious offense.

Counterfeiting official documents, travel permits, Watch tokens, and guild licenses carries severe penalties.

Smuggling, piracy, murder-for-hire, and slave trading are illegal, though enforcement often varies depending on the influence of those involved.

The city permits worship of most deities, provided religious practices do not threaten public safety or disrupt commerce.

Visitors entering the city are expected to pay tolls and comply with inspections when requested by city authorities.

Although these laws appear strict on paper, practical enforcement often depends on wealth, status, and political connections.
''';

const String patriars = '''
The patriars are Baldur's Gate's noble and wealthy elite.

Most patriar families trace their influence back generations and control vast commercial interests, political networks, and valuable properties.

Patriars reside primarily in the Upper City, where they enjoy privileges unavailable to ordinary citizens.

Their estates are protected by walls, private guards, servants, and the constant presence of the Watch.

Many patriars rarely interact with common citizens except through employees, agents, or business representatives.

This isolation has fostered resentment among lower social classes, who often view patriars as arrogant, detached, and indifferent to suffering.

Some nobles embrace these stereotypes, living lives of luxury and excess while exploiting those beneath them.

Others quietly support charitable causes, fund community projects, or attempt to improve conditions for ordinary citizens.

Unfortunately, openly advocating for reform can damage a patriar's reputation among their peers, forcing many well-meaning nobles to act discreetly.

Political rivalries among patriar families shape much of Baldur's Gate's culture, economics, and government.
''';

const String prominentPatriarFamilies = '''
Numerous families dominate the social and economic landscape of Baldur's Gate.

The Belt family is known for horse breeding and trade.

Bormul maintains connections to Amnian nobility and profits from southern mining and vineyards.

Caldwell controls many of the city's art collections and museums.

Dlusker owns textile mills and slaughterhouses despite suffering significant financial troubles.

Durinbold maintains ties to Waterdeep's nobility and oversees extensive sheep herds.

Eltan traces its heritage to the founder of the Flaming Fist.

Eomane profits from perfume production and lamp-oil manufacturing.

Gist dominates the local dye trade.

Guthmere controls major butchery and tannery operations.

Hhune maintains influential relationships with nobles throughout the region.

Hlath owns popular cafés but struggles with gambling debts.

Hullhollyn and Irlentree both control merchant fleets and maintain trade agreements.

Jannath profits from copper and tin mines.

Jhasso holds interests in the Seven Suns Trading Coster.

Linnacker draws wealth from gem mines.

Miyar specializes in wagons, caravans, and overland trade.

Nurthammas finances maritime ventures.

Oathoon imports wines and spirits.

Oberon owns many of the city's dry docks.

Provoss suffered severe losses following agricultural disasters.

Ravenshade trades in luxury goods including gems, jewelry, and dyes.

Redlocks secretly supports smuggling and piracy.

Rillyn operates a renowned martial training school.

Sashenstar possesses holdings in shipping, mining, and textiles.

Shattershield, a shield dwarf family, is the only non-human patriar house and played a crucial role in constructing the city's original defenses.

Tillerturn owns large quantities of rental property.

Vammas controls trade connected to Chult.

Vannath gained status after fleeing Neverwinter and marrying into local nobility.

Vanthampur specializes in civic engineering and infrastructure projects.

Whitburn owns valuable slate quarries east of the city.
''';

const String economyAndTrade = '''
Baldur's Gate owes much of its power and prosperity to commerce.

Located along major trade routes and possessing a large harbor on the Sea of Swords, the city serves as one of the most important economic centers on the western coast.

Merchants transport goods north toward Waterdeep and Neverwinter, south toward Amn, east along the Chionthar River, and across the sea to distant lands.

Although the city exports fish, salt, and related products, its greatest source of wealth comes from facilitating trade itself.

Warehouses, guild halls, docks, marketplaces, and counting houses dominate large sections of the city.

Virtually anything can be bought or sold in Baldur's Gate, including rare artifacts, magical items, secret information, political favors, and illegal services.

Profit often outweighs morality. Citizens frequently tolerate questionable behavior provided commerce remains uninterrupted.

This emphasis on wealth attracts entrepreneurs and adventurers alike, but it also fuels corruption, smuggling, and organized crime.
''';

const String professionalGuilds = '''
Most skilled professions within Baldur's Gate are organized into guilds.

Guild membership provides legitimacy, training, networking opportunities, and protection.

Official guilds operate under city charters and maintain recognized authority over their trades.

Unlicensed workers often struggle to compete with established guild members.

Guilds influence prices, standards, apprenticeships, and employment opportunities.

Many guilds maintain close relationships with local crews and neighborhood organizations.

An attack on one guild member may provoke retaliation from an entire professional community.

Most guild activity occurs within the Lower City, though their highest-paying clients are usually patriars living in the Upper City.

This arrangement generates resentment among common laborers, who often produce goods they cannot personally afford.

Despite complaints, guilds remain an essential part of Baldur's Gate's economy and social structure.
''';

const String religion = '''
Religion in Baldur's Gate is remarkably diverse. Citizens worship dozens of deities from across Faerûn, and the city's authorities generally tolerate all faiths so long as they obey the law and avoid disrupting trade.

Temples serve not only as places of worship but also as community centers, charitable organizations, and political institutions.

The city's practical attitude toward religion reflects its commercial nature. Most citizens care less about what deity a person worships and more about whether that person pays debts, honors agreements, and avoids causing trouble.

This atmosphere has allowed both benevolent and sinister faiths to establish a presence within the city.
''';

const String gond = '''
Followers of Gond are highly respected among craftsmen, engineers, inventors, and artisans.

The faith encourages innovation and celebrates technological progress. Temples dedicated to Gond often function as workshops where new devices, tools, and techniques are developed.

Many guild members maintain close relationships with Gond's clergy, and successful inventions can bring significant wealth and influence.

Because Baldur's Gate values commerce and practical solutions, Gond's teachings fit comfortably within the city's culture.
''';

const String helm = '''
Helm's worshipers emphasize vigilance, duty, responsibility, and protection.

Guards, soldiers, watchmen, and bodyguards commonly seek Helm's guidance.

His clergy are respected for their discipline and reliability, often serving as mediators during disputes.

Although not as politically influential as some other faiths, Helm's church maintains a strong reputation among citizens who value order and stability.
''';

const String ilmater = '''
The followers of Ilmater dedicate themselves to relieving suffering wherever it is found.

Their temples provide shelter, food, healing, and emotional support to the poor, sick, and forgotten.

Many citizens view Ilmater's clergy as among the most genuinely compassionate people in Baldur's Gate.

They frequently work in dangerous districts where poverty, crime, and violence create constant hardship.
''';

const String oghma = '''
Oghma's faithful preserve and distribute knowledge.

Libraries, scholars, scribes, historians, and researchers often maintain ties to his church.

Information is a valuable commodity in Baldur's Gate, making Oghma's clergy surprisingly influential despite their generally neutral role in politics.

